 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 17,
   "y": 12,
   "text": "LANCASTER CASTLE. A fortress above the River Lune for nearly a thousand years, and a working prison until 2011. The Pendle witches were tried here in 1612, a sad chapter. The tour guides have a joke about the stairs."
  },
  {
   "x": 13,
   "y": 14,
   "text": "THE PRIORY. The parish church stands on Roman and Saxon ground, next door to the castle, which is the sort of arrangement you get when a town has been busy for two thousand years."
  },
  {
   "x": 45,
   "y": 18,
   "text": "THE LANCASTER CANAL. Opened in 1797, with no locks for most of its length. Barges carried coal and limestone to Kendal. Now they carry holidaymakers and sandwiches. The towpath is flat, which is the canal's nicest feature."
  },
  {
   "x": 31,
   "y": 34,
   "text": "THE LUNE. A fast, wide river that gave Lancaster its name (Lune Castle). Ships once sailed up it to the Old Port, full of sugar and rum and cotton. The quay has been quiet for a century, which makes it a good place to sit and eat chips."
  },
  {
   "x": 12,
   "y": 34,
   "text": "MILLENNIUM BRIDGE. Opened in 2001, a footbridge shaped like a sail. It's the best place to watch the tide come in at dusk. Take your time. Everyone in Lancaster does."
  }
 ],
